<DocsLayout
  title="Configuration"
  pageKicker="Reference"
  pageTitle="Configuration"
  description="Bridge is configured through a single config.yaml file. This is the complete schema — every field, type, default, and behavior, validated by Zod at load time."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="file-location">File location</h2>
  <p>
    Bridge looks for the config in this order:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>The <code>CONFIG_PATH</code> environment variable, if set</li>
    <li><code>config.yaml</code> in the current working directory</li>
    <li>The bundled default config inside the package (Docker image / npm package)</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    For production deployments, set <code>CONFIG_PATH=/etc/bridge/config.yaml</code> in the systemd unit and keep the file owned by the <code>bridge</code> system user with mode <code>0640</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="top-level">Top-level shape</h2>
  <p>The config has three top-level sections, all required:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code>lab:                  # Lab metadata + global defaults
  ...

services:             # Map of service id → service definition
  apparatus: ...
  chimera-api: ...

profiles:             # Map of profile name → service subset
  full-lab: ...
  testing: ...</code></pre>

  <p>The whole file is validated with <a href="https://zod.dev">Zod</a> at load time. Missing or malformed fields fail loudly with a structured error.</p>

  <h2 id="lab">lab</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string</td><td><code>"Lab"</code></td><td>Display name shown in the dashboard header.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>shutdownGracePeriodMs</code></td><td>integer (ms)</td><td><code>10000</code></td><td>How long to wait after SIGTERM before sending SIGKILL.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <pre is:raw><code>lab:
  name: "Production Lab"
  shutdownGracePeriodMs: 15000</code></pre>

  <h2 id="services">services</h2>
  <p>
    A map of service id → service definition. The id is what you'll see in API URLs (<code>/api/services/:id/start</code>) and what you reference in <code>profiles[*].services</code> and other services' <code>dependencies</code> arrays.
  </p>

  <pre is:raw><code>services:
  apparatus:               # ← service id
    name: "Apparatus"
    cwd: "/opt/apparatus"
    command: "node"
    args: ["dist/server.js"]
    # ...</code></pre>

  <p>Service ids must be unique. Convention is lowercase-kebab-case.</p>

  <h2 id="service-fields">Service fields</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>name</code></td><td>string</td><td>yes</td><td>Display name shown on the service card.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>cwd</code></td><td>string</td><td>yes</td><td>Working directory for the spawned process. Supports env substitution.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>command</code></td><td>string</td><td>yes</td><td>Executable to spawn (e.g. <code>node</code>, <code>pnpm</code>, <code>python</code>).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>args</code></td><td>string[]</td><td>no (default <code>[]</code>)</td><td>Arguments to pass to the command.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>healthCheck</code></td><td>object</td><td>yes</td><td>HTTP health probe configuration. See <a href="#health-check">healthCheck</a>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>ports</code></td><td>object</td><td>yes</td><td>Map of port-label → port number. Used for display + UI links.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>env</code></td><td>object</td><td>no (default <code is:raw>{}</code>)</td><td>Map of env var → value. Layered on top of process env.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>readyPattern</code></td><td>regex string</td><td>no</td><td>Regex matched against stdout to detect readiness. Faster than waiting for health checks.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>dependencies</code></td><td>string[]</td><td>no (default <code>[]</code>)</td><td>Service ids that must be running before this one starts.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>Example service</h3>
  <pre is:raw><code>services:
  apparatus:
    name: "Apparatus"
    cwd: "$DEV_ROOT/Apparatus"
    command: "pnpm"
    args: ["dev:server"]
    healthCheck:
      url: "http://127.0.0.1:8090/healthz"
      intervalMs: 5000
      timeoutMs: 3000
    ports:
      http1: 8090
      http2: 8443
      grpc: 50051
    env:
      DEMO_MODE: "true"
      LOG_LEVEL: "info"
    readyPattern: "server listening"
    dependencies: []</code></pre>

  <h2 id="health-check">healthCheck</h2>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>url</code></td><td>URL string</td><td>—</td><td>HTTP endpoint to poll. Must be a valid absolute URL.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>intervalMs</code></td><td>integer (ms)</td><td><code>5000</code></td><td>How often to poll while the service is running.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>timeoutMs</code></td><td>integer (ms)</td><td><code>3000</code></td><td>Per-request timeout before the poll counts as a failure.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Any 2xx response is considered healthy. Non-2xx, network errors, and timeouts are all "unhealthy."</p>

  <h2 id="profiles">profiles</h2>
  <p>A map of profile name → object with a description and a service id list:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code>profiles:
  full-lab:
    description: "Full security testing stack"
    services: ["apparatus", "chimera-api", "chimera-web", "crucible"]

  testing:
    description: "Apparatus + Chimera API + Crucible (no frontend, no WAF)"
    services: ["apparatus", "chimera-api", "crucible"]</code></pre>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Field</th><th>Type</th><th>Required</th><th>Description</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>description</code></td><td>string</td><td>yes</td><td>Shown in the dashboard's profile launcher dropdown.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>services</code></td><td>string[]</td><td>yes</td><td>Service ids to bring up. Their <code>dependencies</code> are honored automatically.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Profile names must be 1–64 characters of letters, digits, hyphens, or underscores (validated server-side on POST <code>/api/profiles</code>).</p>

  <h2 id="env-substitution">Env substitution</h2>
  <p>
    Any string value in the config can reference environment variables using <code>$VAR</code> or <code is:raw>${VAR}</code> syntax. The substitution happens once at config load time using the server process's own environment.
  </p>
  <pre is:raw><code>services:
  apparatus:
    cwd: "$DEV_ROOT/Apparatus"            # → /home/nick/dev/Apparatus
    env:
      LOG_DIR: "${HOME}/logs/apparatus"   # → /home/nick/logs/apparatus</code></pre>

  <p>If a referenced variable is undefined, it expands to the empty string. There's no default-value syntax — set the variable in your shell, your <code>.env</code>, or your systemd unit.</p>

  <h2 id="ready-pattern">readyPattern semantics</h2>
  <p>
    A service is considered <em>ready</em> when one of two things happens:
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>A line on stdout matches <code>readyPattern</code> (interpreted as a JavaScript regex), <em>or</em></li>
    <li>The first health check returns 2xx (only used as a fallback when no <code>readyPattern</code> is configured)</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Until then, the service shows as <code>starting</code> on the dashboard and dependents won't begin starting. Bridge waits up to 60 seconds for readiness; after that the service is marked <code>crashed</code> and the operator is notified.
  </p>

  <p>Common patterns:</p>
  <pre is:raw><code>readyPattern: "server listening"
readyPattern: "Listening at|Worker spawned"
readyPattern: "ready in|Local:"
readyPattern: "Admin HTTP server listening on"</code></pre>

  <h2 id="hot-reload">Hot reload</h2>
  <p>
    Bridge watches the config file. When you save it (either through the in-browser editor or with your text editor), the config is re-validated and the in-memory definition is updated. Specifically:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Running services keep running</strong> with their original config — Bridge never silently restarts a process under you.</li>
    <li><strong>New service definitions</strong> become available immediately — start them from the dashboard.</li>
    <li><strong>Edited service definitions</strong> take effect on the next start. The dashboard surfaces a "config changed since start" indicator on the affected card.</li>
    <li><strong>Removed services</strong> stay running until manually stopped, then disappear from the grid.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A <code>CONFIG_RELOADED</code> message is broadcast on the WebSocket so all connected dashboards refresh in sync.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sample">Full sample config</h2>
  <p>Below is a working config for the default Atlas Crew Security stack. Copy this as a starting point.</p>

  <pre is:raw><code>lab:
  name: "Inferno Lab"
  shutdownGracePeriodMs: 10000

services:
  apparatus:
    name: "Apparatus"
    cwd: "$DEV_ROOT/Apparatus"
    command: "pnpm"
    args: ["dev:server"]
    healthCheck:
      url: "http://127.0.0.1:8090/healthz"
      intervalMs: 5000
      timeoutMs: 3000
    ports:
      http1: 8090
      http2: 8443
      grpc: 50051
      tcp-echo: 9090
      udp-echo: 9001
      redis-mock: 6380
    env:
      DEMO_MODE: "true"
      PORT_TCP: "9090"
      PORT_REDIS: "6380"
    readyPattern: "server listening"
    dependencies: []

  chimera-api:
    name: "Chimera API"
    cwd: "$DEV_ROOT/Chimera"
    command: "pnpm"
    args: ["nx", "run", "chimera-api:start"]
    healthCheck:
      url: "http://127.0.0.1:8880/health"
      intervalMs: 5000
      timeoutMs: 3000
    ports:
      http: 8880
    env:
      DEMO_MODE: "full"
      APPARATUS_ENABLED: "true"
      APPARATUS_BASE_URL: "http://127.0.0.1:8090"
    readyPattern: "Listening at|Worker spawned"
    dependencies: ["apparatus"]

  chimera-web:
    name: "Chimera Web"
    cwd: "$DEV_ROOT/Chimera"
    command: "pnpm"
    args: ["nx", "run", "vuln-web:start"]
    healthCheck:
      url: "http://127.0.0.1:5175"
      intervalMs: 10000
      timeoutMs: 3000
    ports:
      http: 5175
    env: {}
    readyPattern: "ready in|Local:"
    dependencies: ["chimera-api"]

  crucible:
    name: "Crucible"
    cwd: "$DEV_ROOT/Crucible"
    command: "pnpm"
    args: ["dev"]
    healthCheck:
      url: "http://127.0.0.1:3001/health"
      intervalMs: 5000
      timeoutMs: 3000
    ports:
      backend: 3001
      frontend: 3000
    env:
      CRUCIBLE_TARGET_URL: "http://127.0.0.1:8880"
    readyPattern: "Demo Dashboard server running"
    dependencies: ["chimera-api"]

profiles:
  full-lab:
    description: "All managed services"
    services: ["apparatus", "chimera-api", "chimera-web", "crucible"]

  chimera-stack:
    description: "Apparatus + Chimera (API + Web)"
    services: ["apparatus", "chimera-api", "chimera-web"]

  testing:
    description: "Apparatus + Chimera API + Crucible"
    services: ["apparatus", "chimera-api", "crucible"]

  apparatus-only:
    description: "Apparatus standalone"
    services: ["apparatus"]</code></pre>
</DocsLayout>
